\begin{abstract}
Urban disaster response unfolds as a coupled social process: residents receive
unequal information, negotiate within households, revise plans after
disruption, and act under shared physical constraints. Generative agents
produce linguistically plausible behavior, but social simulation requires
behavior that holds under informational, social, and physical
constraints: what a resident believes, what a household has committed to, and
what the world permits are three different states. We present
\emph{DisasterSociety}, a generative agent-based simulation framework built on
this separation. A resident language model observes, remembers, plans, and
communicates, producing structured intentions; a household layer validates
accepted proposals into versioned commitments; and a deterministic world
arbitrates intentions against one authoritative snapshot, returning
execution outcomes as feedback. In a Carr-Fire-informed
controlled scenario, a paired twelve-seed ablation
separates mechanism function from downstream necessity: memory, feedback,
planning, and interaction operate at their own interfaces, yet only
feedback and interaction show consistent downstream effects---removing
feedback leaves residents acting on a stale pre-closure route view and
raises closed-route rejections by 6.542 per household, and removing
interaction eliminates coordinated multi-adult departure. A deterministic reference
policy establishes the scenario's feasibility ceiling, a bounded
seven-model replication shows the two effects hold in direction across model
families (feedback in all seven, interaction in six), and instruction
paraphrases reproduce them; process studies at 24
and 100 households yield multi-step coordination and departure with
zero constraint violations, with level gaps against survey references reported
explicitly. DisasterSociety offers an inspectable framework to study how
heterogeneous resident decisions shape household and collective
disaster response.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Generative agents, multi-agent simulation, urban disaster response, household
coordination, social simulation, event-driven simulation, mechanism ablation.
\end{IEEEkeywords}


